---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "../consts";
import { breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema } from "../lib/schema";

const description =
  "On design memory — why preserving the reasoning behind past decisions, not just the drawings, turns experience into an active part of future design work.";

const jsonLd = [
  breadcrumbSchema([
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Design with Memory", path: "/design-with-memory" },
  ]),
  articleSchema({
    headline: "Design with Memory",
    description,
    path: "/design-with-memory",
  }),
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Design with Memory" description={description} path="/design-with-memory" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-10 md:px-10 md:pt-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
      <a href="/about" class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-stone hover:text-ink">
        ← About
      </a>
      <p class="coord-mark mt-6">Notes on memory</p>
      <h1 class="mt-3 font-display text-4xl text-ink md:text-5xl">Design with Memory</h1>
      <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-ink/75">{description}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="px-6 pb-16 md:px-10 md:pb-24">
    <div class="mx-auto mb-12 max-w-3xl border border-stone-light/70 bg-paper p-6">
      <p class="italic text-red-600">
        If you are interested in designing through relationships, context, and meaning, there is
        no need to settle for a "design without memories." I invite you to visit{" "}
        <a href="https://www.tunakocayurt.com" class="underline hover:no-underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          www.tunakocayurt.com
        </a>. I hope that if you take the time to read a brief article on design memory, you will
        see the difference.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-content mx-auto max-w-3xl text-ink/85">
      <p>
        Design is often thought of as the act of creating something new. Yet, powerful design
        draws sustenance not only from innovation but also from its relationship with the past.
        When designing a product, space, exhibition, or communication system, the systematic
        preservation of prior decisions, experiments, user behaviors, failures, and contextual
        information renders the design process more informed. Therefore, building a design
        memory is not merely about keeping an archive; it is about transforming past experience
        into an active component of future decisions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Design memory can consist of various layers. The first layer comprises drawings,
        sketches, models, technical details, material choices, and production files. However,
        these alone are insufficient. What is truly valuable is recording the reasons behind
        these decisions. Why was a particular material chosen? Why was a solution abandoned? At
        what point did the user encounter difficulties? Under what economic, cultural, or
        technical constraints was the design developed? When the answers to these questions are
        lost, only the final result remains. Yet, design knowledge often resides more in the
        decision-making process than in the outcome itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        Integrating this memory into the design process prevents the need to constantly start
        from scratch on new projects. Designers can interpret past examples not merely as formal
        references, but as a network of decisions. For instance, insights—such as how a
        previously used display system affected visitor flow, which artworks caused issues with
        a specific lighting solution, or what consequences a particular production detail yielded
        in the field—can be transformed into directly applicable knowledge for new projects.
      </p>
      <p>
        The crucial point here is that memory should not render the designer dependent on the
        past. Memory should generate context, not a template. Instead of simply repeating old
        solutions, one must understand the conditions under which those solutions emerged. In
        this way, the designer does not copy the past; it generates a new decision by drawing
        comparisons with the past.
      </p>
      <p>
        Digital tools and artificial intelligence can further enhance this process. Hundreds of
        project files, meeting notes, drawings, visuals, and technical documents can be linked to
        create a searchable design memory. When a designer works on a new project, the system can
        display decisions made, materials used, or issues encountered under similar past
        conditions. Thus, the archive transforms from a passive repository into an active design
        partner.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ultimately, design memory serves as a decision-making infrastructure bridging the past
        and the future. When well-established, it accelerates the design process, minimizes
        redundancy, preserves institutional knowledge, and ensures that design relies not merely
        on intuition but on accumulated experience. Design thus ceases to be a singular act of
        creation; it evolves into a living system that continuously learns, updates itself, and
        draws upon past experiences.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto mt-16 max-w-3xl border-t border-stone-light/70 pt-10">
      <p class="coord-mark">Where this leads</p>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-xl text-ink/75">
        It's why {SITE.name} keeps the reasoning behind a project, not just its drawings — every
        decision on a new commission draws on what we learned building the last one.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-3">
        <a
          href="/practice"
          class="inline-block bg-ink px-6 py-3 font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-paper hover:bg-patina"
        >
          See our practice
        </a>
        <a
          href="/non-places"
          class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-patina hover:underline"
        >
          Continue reading: Non-Places →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose-content :global(p) {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .prose-content :global(p:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
